{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=waikato}}
'''Hamilton West''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]]. It has been held by [[Tama Potaka]] MP<ref>{{cite web |title=Gaurav Sharma |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/members-of-parliament/sharma-gaurav/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201023200945/https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/members-of-parliament/sharma-gaurav/ |url-status=dead |archive-date=23 October 2020 |website=New Zealand Parliament |access-date=19 October 2020 }}</ref> of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] since the [[2022 Hamilton West by-election|2022 by-election]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sowman-Lund |first1=Stewart |title=Rogue MP Gaurav Sharma quits parliament, triggers byelection |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/18-10-2022/gaurav-sharma-quits-parliament-triggers-byelection |access-date=18 October 2022 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=18 October 2022}}</ref>

Hamilton West is regarded as a [[Bellwether (politics)|bellwether seat]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Hamilton West – Electorate Profile |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/hamilton-west-electorate-profile/ |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=19 October 2022 |date=27 April 2021}}</ref> In 17 of the 18 general elections since the electorate's creation, the party that has won the plurality of seats nationally has won Hamilton West. The sole exception was in [[1993 New Zealand general election|1993]], when [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] won the electorate but [[New Zealand National Party|National]] won the plurality of the seats.

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Hamilton West.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
Through an amendment in the Electoral Act in 1965, the number of electorates in the [[South Island]] was fixed at 25, an increase of one since the 1962 electoral redistribution.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=108, 111, 112}} It was accepted that through the more rapid population growth in the [[North Island]], the number of its electorates would continue to increase, and to keep proportionality, three new electorates were allowed for in the 1967 electoral redistribution for the next election.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=111}} In the North Island, five electorates were newly created (including Hamilton West) and one electorate was reconstituted while three electorates were abolished (including {{NZ electorate link|Hamilton}}).{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=107, 111}} In the South Island, three electorates were newly created and one electorate was reconstituted while three electorates were abolished.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=108, 112}} The overall effect of the required changes was highly disruptive to existing electorates, with all but three electorates having their boundaries altered.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=111f}} These changes came into effect with the {{NZ election link|1969}}.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=111}}

The former Hamilton electorate had existed since {{NZ election link year|1922}} and had covered the urban area.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=106}} When it was replaced by Hamilton West for the 1969 election, only part of the urban area was included but also rural land stretching all the way to the west coast.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=110}} Most of the rural land had previously been part of the {{NZ electorate link|Waipa}} electorate.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=106, 110}} The town of [[Raglan, New Zealand|Raglan]] was the north-western point of the electorate, and was surprisingly not located in the {{NZ electorate link|Raglan}} electorate. In the south-west, the electorate stretched as far as just north of the [[Kāwhia Harbour]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=110}}

The 1972 electoral redistribution significantly reduced the size of the Hamilton West electorate, and all the rural land mostly transferred to the Raglan electorate.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=110, 114, 115}} The additional electorate of {{NZ electorate link|Hamilton East}} was created at the same time. These changes came into effect with the {{NZ election link|1972}}{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=115}} and since then, the electorate has been mainly urban, and has covered the western part of the city of [[Hamilton, New Zealand|Hamilton]], including the [[Hamilton Central|CBD]], [[Hamilton North, New Zealand|Hamilton North]], [[Hamilton West, New Zealand|Hamilton West]], the suburbs of [[Te Rapa]], [[Pukete]], [[St Andrews, Hamilton|St Andrews]], [[Beerescourt]], [[Nawton, New Zealand|Nawton]], [[Grandview Heights, New Zealand|Grandview Heights]], [[Western Heights, Hamilton|Western Heights]], [[Dinsdale, New Zealand|Dinsdale]], [[Temple View]], [[Frankton, Hamilton|Frankton]], [[Maeroa]], [[Hamilton Lake (suburb)|Hamilton Lake]], [[Melville, New Zealand|Melville]], [[Bader, New Zealand|Bader]], [[Glenview, New Zealand|Glenview]] and [[Fitzroy, Hamilton|Fitzroy]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/hamilton-west-electorate-profile/ |title=Hamilton West - Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 22 December 2022 |access-date= 22 September 2025}}</ref>

The [[Waikato River]] divides the city in half and forms the boundary between the Hamilton East and Hamilton West electorates, except in the north where Hamilton West covers part of [[Flagstaff, Hamilton|Flagstaff]] on the east bank. Hamilton West also borders the rural electorates of {{NZ electorate link|Waikato}} to the north and west, and [[Taranaki-King Country]] to the south.

The 2020 electoral redistribution saw the area north of Borman Road, including [[Horsham Downs, New Zealand|Horsham Downs]], ceded to {{NZ electorate link|Waikato}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2020 |date=17 April 2020}}</ref>

==History==
The first representative of Hamilton West was [[Leslie Munro]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]], who had since the {{NZ election link|1963}} represented the Waipa electorate.{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=222}} When Hamilton West became an urban electorate in 1972, Munro retired and the electorate was won by [[Dorothy Jelicich]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]].{{sfn|Wilson|1985|pp=208, 222}} Jelicich was defeated after one term in the {{NZ election link|1975}} by National's [[Mike Minogue (politician)|Mike Minogue]].{{sfn|Wilson|1985|pp=208, 220}}

